to be the new TanStack app structure\\n- They\\'re on branch \"experimental/tanstack\"\\n- Main branch is \"main\"\\n\\nThe user mentions:\\n1. Deleting the Next.js app (which they already have on remote)\\n2. Rebasing to the TanStack app while keeping Next.js as a deprecated branch\\n\\nThe options they mention:\\n- Delete the Next.js app from the repo (keeping it on remote)\\n- Rebase the tanstack work onto main and keep Next.js as a deprecated branch\\n\\nI should provide advice on the best Git workflow for this situation. The key considerations are:\\n- They want to preserve the Next.js version somewhere\\n- They want to simplify the current repo\\n- They\\'ve done work on experimental/tanstack branch\\n\\nLet me think about the options:\\n\\nOption 1: Merge experimental/tanstack into main (after cleanup)\\n- Pros: Preserves history, straightforward\\n- Cons: Next.js files will still be in history but deleted\\n\\nOption 2: Rebase experimental/tanstack onto main\\n- Pros: Clean linear history\\n- Cons: Might be complex if there are many commits\\n\\nOption 3: Create a new branch with just the TanStack version\\n- Could use git worktree or separate branch\\n\\nOption 4: Tag the current main, then merge tanstack work\\n- This preserves the Next.js version point in history\\n\\nI think the best approach would be:\\n1. Tag or branch the current main as a reference point for the Next.js version\\n\\n\\n2. I\\'ll merge the experimental TanStack branch into main, carefully cleaning up any remaining Next.js artifacts. I\\'ll focus on removing unnecessary files and ensuring a clean transition. This approach lets me preserve the original Next.js version while moving forward with the new implementation.\\n\\nThe strategy involves creating a clear historical marker, then cleanly integrating the new work. I\\'ll ensure all Next.js specific files are removed and the project structure reflects the TanStack approach. This method provides a clean slate while maintaining a complete project history.'}, {'type': 'text', 'text': \" Looking at your situation, I'd recommend this clean approach:\\n\\n1. **Tag the current Next.js version** before merging:\\n   ```bash\\n   git tag nextjs-legacy-final\\n   git push origin nextjs-legacy-final\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Clean up your experimental/tanstack branch** - ensure all Next.js files are properly deleted and the TanStack structure is clean\\n\\n3. **Merge to main** (not rebase, to preserve your branch history):\\n   ```bash\\n   git checkout main\\n   git merge experimental/tanstack --no-ff\\n   ```\\n\\nThis gives you:\\n- \u2705 Clean main branch with only TanStack code\\n- \u2705 Preserved Next.js version that you can checkout anytime: `git checkout nextjs-legacy-final`\\n- \u2705 Full git history of your migration work\\n- \u2705 No complex rebasing needed\\n\\nThe tag acts as an immutable snapshot of the Next.js version without cluttering your repo with a long-lived deprecated branch. You can always create a branch from that tag later if needed.\\n\\nWant me to help you execute this plan, or would you prefer to handle the git operations yourself?\"}], 'reasoning_content': None}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'you could start applying this yourself as long as it does not ruin the repo'}], 'reasoning_content': None}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': ' '}, {'type': 'thinking', 'thinking': ' The user wants me to apply the plan I suggested, but with caution. Let me break down what needs to be done:\\n\\n1. First, I should check the current state of the repo more thoroughly\\n2. Create a tag for the current state as \"nextjs-legacy-final\"\\n3. Ensure the experimental/tanstack branch is clean (all Next.js files deleted)\\n4. Merge it to main\\n\\nLet me start by exploring the repo structure to understand better what we\\'re working with. '}, {'type': 'text', 'text': \" I'll help you apply this plan carefully. Let me start by exploring the repository str