te project history.'}, {'type': 'text', 'text': \" Looking at your situation, I'd recommend this clean approach:\\n\\n1. **Tag the current Next.js version** before merging:\\n   ```bash\\n   git tag nextjs-legacy-final\\n   git push origin nextjs-legacy-final\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Clean up your experimental/tanstack branch** - ensure all Next.js files are properly deleted and the TanStack structure is clean\\n\\n3. **Merge to main** (not rebase, to preserve your branch history):\\n   ```bash\\n   git checkout main\\n   git merge experimental/tanstack --no-ff\\n   ```\\n\\nThis gives you:\\n- \u2705 Clean main branch with only TanStack code\\n- \u2705 Preserved Next.js version that you can checkout anytime: `git checkout nextjs-legacy-final`\\n- \u2705 Full git history of your migration work\\n- \u2705 No complex rebasing needed\\n\\nThe tag acts as an immutable snapshot of the Next.js version without cluttering your repo with a long-lived deprecated branch. You can always create a branch from that tag later if needed.\\n\\nWant me to help you execute this plan, or would you prefer to handle the git operations yourself?\"}], 'reasoning_content': None}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'you could start applying this yourself as long as it does not ruin the repo'}], 'reasoning_content': None}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': ' '}, {'type': 'thinking', 'thinking': ' The user wants me to apply the plan I suggested, but with caution. Let me break down what needs to be done:\\n\\n1. First, I should check the current state of the repo more thoroughly\\n2. Create a tag for the current state as \"nextjs-legacy-final\"\\n3. Ensure the experimental/tanstack branch is clean (all Next.js files deleted)\\n4. Merge it to main\\n\\nLet me start by exploring the repo structure to understand better what we\\'re working with. '}, {'type': 'text', 'text': \" I'll help you apply this plan carefully. Let me start by exploring the repository str